  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 339 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nBroad upper ridge remains across the southern US. Closer to the \nsurface, a 30-40 knot low level jet remains from eastern TX into \ncentral Indiana.  This low level jet has prevented the atmosphere \nfrom cooling substantially, and fueled storms across the \nregion. These storms are handled relatively poorly by various \nmodels, but have begun to be recognized by the HRRR/RAP over the\nlast few hours. Expect the threat elevated convection to remain\nthrough the mid morning hours but expect an overall weakening \ntrend as the morning goes on. There is a shortwave across south \ncentral Nebraska as of 08Z, and with ample mid level moisture, \ncould see a renewed threat of elevated showers/thunderstorms \nacross northern Missouri mid-day. With the expected cloud cover \nand showers, expect high temperatures to be moderated a bit. \nConsidered a heat advisory along and south of I-70 (north of the\nexcessive heat warning), but with impacts of cloud cover and \nshowers, opted to just stick with the excessive heat warning \nacross central MO. \n\nCAMs suggest convection redeveloping across the High Plains late \nthis afternoon, pushing east/southeast tonight on the leading edge \nof the thermal ridge. With this activity once again building \ninto a strengthening 40-50 knot low level jet, could see these \nstorms last longer than CAMs suggest leading to another night of\npotential scattered storms. \n\nClearing skies through the day is once again expected to lead to \nanother mild day with highs in the mid and upper 90s with heat \nindices of 105-110 F degrees. Will need to once again consider a\nheat advisory for Tuesday depending on potential clearing. \n\nStationary front across Central Iowa along I-80 may try to work \nsouth as low pressure develops in western KS and gradually pushes \neast.  South of the front, warm and humid conditions are expected to \ncontinue, so location of the front will be key.  Low level jet south \nof the boundary will likely fuel nocturnal thunderstorms on the \nnorth side of boundary Wednesday night. Additional ridge riding \nshortwaves are expected through the late week into the weekend \nleading for additional periodic chances for showers and \nthunderstorms.",
